Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400 align:start position:0%
 
we start this morning with the race to  succeed David Cameron as the  Conservative Party leader and prime  minister today the five candidates are  expected to outline more of their plans  ahead of the first ballot of Tory MPs  which will take place tomorrow the main  developments this morning and the  leadership candidate Andrea led sim will  launch her campaign as she seeks to take  on the frontrunner to resume the  Chancellor George Osborne has pledged to  cut corporation tax to cushion the  impact of leaving the EU and leave  campaigner Boris Johnson calls on the  government to deliver a detailed plan  for brexit let's go live to Westminster  now we can speak to the culture  secretary John Whittingdale who is  supporting Michael Gove very good  morning to you and first question really  why are you supporting him well I've  worked with Michael in government he's  an outstanding record as a minister he  provided extremely strong leadership  during the vote leave campaign and I  know him to be a man of principle and  conviction with experience and I think  he's the right man to take this country  forward and to negotiate our exit from  the European Union let's talk about his  principles I mean he stabbed his running  mate in the back last week the most  spectacular way just hours before he's  about to announce his own leadership  position Boris Johnson I'll go put  himself forward instead well I think  Michael reach look in conclusion having  had discussions with Boris that he  wasn't the man to provide the leadership  nurse Johnson didn't know about that  teamworks I think is a man of great  talent I I'm hugely admired his  commitment to believe cause during the  campaign and I've no doubt but he will  have a very senior role in government in  the future but at this time I think  Michael felt but having talked to him he  wasn't the man to provide the leadership  that we need now therefore I had  previously pressed Michael to put his  own name forward and so I was very  pleased when he did so he could have  chosen a better time to do it i mean  he's known Boris Johnson for 30 years  he's been campaigning to leave the  European Union for month side by side he  spent four days not airing any of his  concerns to the campaign managers Ben  Wallace who was quoted in the paper  today saying it was  late shock to them the morning that Mr  Gove decided to withdraw his support for  Boris Johnson a spectacular betrayal  well I mean I agree with you the timing  wasn't great and it would have been  better to have reached that decision  before but bear in mind firstly that  we'd spent the time campaigning to vote  for a vote to leave the European Union  I'd anything that he would have had any  conversation during the campaign about  what am daft words and indeed the one  thing all of us hoped would happen and I  signed a letter to this effect was that  the Prime Minister would continue and  therefore we wouldn't be plunged  straight into a leadership election so i  think that the subject only really arose  at the very last minute and it was as a  result of those discussions that Michael  sadly reached the conclusion that Boris  wouldn't be the man to take this country  forward the public are always already  suspicious of politicians they already  think that they can't be trusted if Mr  Gove is being seen to put self-interest  ahead of the public into the national  interest that's not going to warm him to  the public is it he's not gonna be able  to win an election as the Prime Minister  well I don't think Michael was putting  self-interest ahead in fact Michael had  said clearly that he was reluctant to  stand that he didn't have an ambition to  become Prime Minister and I think that  it was only because he reached the  conclusion that Boris was not the man to  do so and others like myself had pressed  him to stand that hit then he reached  the decision but he would do so so I  mean I think he is a reluctant prime  minister but I sure I think that's a  point in his favor if you really cared  about the national interest would it not  be better to see a coronation of Theresa  May somebody that would enable the  country to get on with the business of  working out how we're going to withdraw  from the EU rather than losing our  attention and focusing on a messy  potentially leadership battle well we  are a democracy in this country and in  the Conservative Party and actually I  think it's right and I think Teresa  herself has said so it's right that our  party members should have the ultimate  decision as to who should be leader I  don't like the idea of a coronation I  think democratic contests are a good  thing and that it will strengthen  whoever emerges as the winner in which  case would you like to see a snap  general election no we have been in this  position before John Major to go from  Margaret Thatcher Gordon Brown took him  from Tony Blair the Conservative Party  has a mature  30 in Parliament where a parliamentary  democracy as long as we have a majority  in parliament then we will continue to  govern until the next election let's  talk about what Michael Gove actually  stands for I mean if you're supporting  him do you accept being outside the  single market well these are  negotiations which haven't even started  yet I'm not frightened of being outside  the single market but what we want to do  is to have that discussion with our  friends in Europe and work out in the  arrangement well what I would like to  see is our ability to continue to trade  but at the same time we have to regain  control of our borders and stop paying  the money that we currently sent to  Europe those are two things which the  British people are very clearly voted  for and obviously we will be determined  to deliver and how do you plan to regain  control of the borders i mean the  australian style point system or it they  have a higher immigration rate than we  do here it's not going to solve the  problem well what it does mean is if we  brought in a point system is it's a much  fairer way of determining who should  come here instead of have the existing  position where anybody from any European  country can come in without our ability  to stop them which means that we've had  to put in place much stronger controls  against countries outside the european  union under a point system we judge  everybody fairly and on the basis of  what contribution they will make to our  country and when we look at the  withdrawal process is going to both  involve very delicate negotiations with  the European Union with Brussels do you  think that Michael Gove is the right  person to handle those given that if you  talk to any teacher they say that he  wasn't a good negotiation that was from  a position of power where he was able to  push through those reforms and say sorry  guys this is what we're going to do when  we're walking into the EU for those  negotiations were saying we'd really  like to try and stay in the single  market if we can but we're going to have  to lose free movement of people will he  be able to meet the right candidate for  that well I think actually Michael was  an extremely good education secretary I  think he's pretty good Justice Secretary  well he was pushing through some quite  radical reforms and there was opposition  to those but I think they were the right  reforms I think they improved our  education system as I say I know Michael  well I think he's a man of conviction  he's got great experience and obviously  the negotiations which lie ahead will be  done by a team  also have the support of the officials  in doing so will have the best and  brightest as the prime minister said and  it is in the interests of both sides  both the European Union and this country  that we reach a deal and like we've got  the first round of voting taking place  tomorrow at the moment Mr Gove isn't  looking in a particularly strong  position in fact he's losing support by  the day if he loses this leadership  election is his career finished well I  think michael has a great deal to offer  this country and I hope that you'll  continue to serve in any capacity but I  believe he's the best man to lead the  Conservative Party and to become Prime  Minister and therefore I hope that that  will be the outcome of the election and  obviously ultimately it is a decision  for the members of the Conservative  Party in the final round

